export const body = "<p>Games, much like any other form of entertainment, are up to personal tastes. Different types of gamers enjoy different types of games. But there is one thing that gamers unanimously loathe: loading screens. In this article, we will learn the concept of level streaming, which explains the existence of loading times in games.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/1b3e6e63996d16fa7da8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/1b3e6e63996d16fa7da8.webp\" width=\"708\" height=\"553\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/1d1b9389ed21c1cfff7e.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/1d1b9389ed21c1cfff7e.webp\" width=\"605\" height=\"369\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/61de913f1e8d7b0fe528.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/61de913f1e8d7b0fe528.webp\" width=\"957\" height=\"537\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Level streaming refers to how your level is loaded and displayed on screen for the player. In the opposite pictures, you can see that the hardware only streams in a certain amount of tiles around the player. As the player progress through the level, the tiles will be loaded and unloaded accordingly. This restriction affects the design of many games, especially those in past generations. For example, <em>Resident Evil 4</em> had to break the game world into rooms, streaming each one in at a time, limiting the enemy variety per area to keep the loading times short.</p>\n\n<p>Generally, the streaming speed is bound the storage (HDD or SSD) of the operating system. At the same price tag, an SSD will yield much faster speeds than the HDD, but the HDD will provide more storage space.</p>\n\n<p>Over the years, developers have come up with ways to mitigate less-than-ideal streaming speeds. In <em>Jedi Fallen Order</em>, there are some narrow gaps that require that player to squeeze through, basically slowing their movement speed so that assets can be streamed in.</p>\n\n<p>The issue is called “pop-in.” It happens when a game can’t load assets (meshes and textures) into the game world fast enough. As a result, things pop in and out of frames whenever new assets are required.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/1ec48f62fb9f82f210d3.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/1ec48f62fb9f82f210d3.webp\" width=\"763\" height=\"425\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/e59d0f46fdc2fb814853.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/e59d0f46fdc2fb814853.webp\" width=\"641\" height=\"355\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/8eeb75aa171699174b1d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/8eeb75aa171699174b1d.webp\" width=\"847\" height=\"473\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></em></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/b9b91b1ce0d24cd71932.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/b9b91b1ce0d24cd71932.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"686\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Another way is to turn performance-intensive sequences into \"interactive cutscenes.\" Two characters having a conversation on a walk, for example. Or, in the opposite GIF from <em>Final Fantasy VII Remake</em>, the player character is pushed back by the spirits, and thus reduced to shambling. Granted, not every \"walking segment\" like this is designed to mask loading.</p>\n\n<p>Fast travel also results in loading screens, and <em>Spider-Man</em> <em>PS4</em> has an ingenious way to deal with it. Instead of fading to black, the game recontextualized the fast travel as Spider-Man moves from one place to another by subway trains.</p>\n\n<p>Speaking of <em>Spider-Man PS4</em>, here is a demo that showcases the discrepancy in streaming speeds between PS4 and PS5 (which was still under development back then). Because of the inadequate streaming speed of the PS4's HDD,<em> the developers </em>had to limit how fast Spider-Man could swing around the city, lest he advance to beyond the hardware capacity.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/96c579a0a30a3fa5fb2f.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/96c579a0a30a3fa5fb2f.webp\" width=\"517\" height=\"294\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/4e233a5b0d61c7d318cc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/4e233a5b0d61c7d318cc.webp\" width=\"780\" height=\"436\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>As gaming hardware accumulates more horsepower with each generation passed, load times have been increasingly shortened. And the SSD implementation in Xbox Series X and Playstation 5 marks a new epoch of near-instantaneous loading times, demonstrated by the remake of <em>Demon's Souls</em>. Moreover, this new component also ushered in more creative opportunities for game designers, as they're less restricted by insufficient streaming speeds. In 2021, <em>Ratchet and Clank: Rift Apart </em>gave us a glimpse into this promising future by delivering an experience built around inter-dimensional traveling.</p>\n\n<p>However, such hardware improvement doesn't necessarily eliminate the need for level streaming. It simply means developers can now transfer data from the storage to the memory more efficiently. If you want to peek into what's next for level streaming, you can start with the World Partition system in Unreal Engine 5.</p>";
